Assertions:
[REL] BAL_1 is greater than BAL_0 — member A's own order moved the balance they read {SPEC}
[REL] BAL_B_SEES equals BAL_1 — member B, who has ordered nothing, reads the figure member A's order produced; this single equality IS the pooling claim, and it fails if points are still keyed per user {SPEC}
[REL] BAL_2 is greater than BAL_B_SEES — member B's own order moved the same one pool {SPEC}
[STATE] ORDER_SPEND exists — the second member could SPEND what the first member earned, which is the half of the promise a read alone cannot prove {SPEC}
[DOM] a points-history row attributable to ORDER_SPEND is present, its rendered amount magnitude equals PTS_SPENT, and its operation-type cell denotes a REDEMPTION rather than an earning — the redemption debited the shared pool rather than a private one. Read the rendered operation label verbatim; never match an invented literal. Do NOT assert that BAL_3 fell below BAL_2: BL-LOY-010 forbids a points-only cart, so this order necessarily carries an earning cash line and the NET balance movement may be upward {SPEC}
[REL] BAL_A_FINAL equals BAL_3 — member A sees member B's spending, so the two members are reading one number in both directions {SPEC}
[STATE] ORDER_A, ORDER_B and ORDER_SPEND are all present and distinct — the journey placed three real, separate orders {SPEC}

[DOM] the transactions list on /account/points-history renders at least one row for member B although member B caused none of them — in Organization mode the ledger a buyer reads is the company's {SPEC}
[DOM] a rendered row corresponding to an order member A placed is present in the transactions list, identified by its order number or its amount {SPEC}
[DOM] every rendered row carries a field that identifies the member who caused it — a buyer must be able to tell their own purchases from a colleague's {BL}
[REL] ROWS_B exceeds the number of transactions member B's own orders can account for — the extra rows are the colleagues' {SPEC}

[STATE] ORG_2 is not ORG_1 — the switch actually changed the active organization {OBSERVED}
[REL] verify whether the figure on the page changes with the active organization: BAL_ORG_2 reflects the organization named by ORG_2 and not the pool shown under ORG_1 {OBSERVED}
[REL] BAL_ORG_1_AGAIN equals BAL_ORG_1 — switching away and back returns the same figure, so nothing leaked or was cached across the two contexts {OBSERVED}
[DOM] no row listed under ORG_1 is present in the transactions list under ORG_2 {OBSERVED}

[DOM] an element identifying WHOSE balance is shown is present on /account/points-history — in Organization mode a shared company pool must not be presented with wording that attributes it to the signed-in buyer alone {DOC}
[DOM] an element identifying WHOSE progress and rewards are shown is present on /account/missions {DOC}
[DOM] at least one element distinguishing the Organization-mode presentation from the Customer-mode one is present {SPEC}
[DOM] no label claiming exclusivity over a balance colleagues can spend is present on either page {DOC}
[REL] the possessive wording on /account/points-history and on /account/missions agrees — a buyer must not be told the balance is theirs on one page and the company's on the other {SPEC}

[DOM] a page-level balance value is present and BAL_ORG is greater than 0 — the case is vacuous against an empty pool, so a zero here is BLOCKED rather than a pass {SPEC}
[REL] BAL_CUST is less than BAL_ORG — after the merchant changes the setting, the page shows the buyer's own scope rather than the company pool it showed a moment earlier {SPEC}
[REL] BAL_RESTORED equals BAL_ORG — restoring the setting restores the figure, so the page is reading the live scope and not a figure frozen at first load {SPEC}
[DOM] a numeric balance is present at every one of the three readings — never an error state, never an endless skeleton {SPEC}

[DOM] a page-level balance is present on the account page and BAL_SHOWN is greater than 0 {SPEC}
[DOM] the cart renders no insufficient-balance message for a buyer whose account page shows a balance covering the line — an organization-scoped store setting must not make a personal shopper's points unspendable {SPEC}
[DOM] the 'Place Order' control is enabled on the cart {SPEC}
[STATE] OUTCOME is an order confirmation with an order number, not a blocking message {SPEC}
[REL] if the cart DOES name an available figure, it equals BAL_SHOWN — the balance the buyer is shown and the balance checkout honours are one number {SPEC}

[STATE] ORG_2 is not ORG_1 — the switch actually changed the active company, without which every comparison below is vacuous {SPEC}
[REL] BAL_ORG2_AFTER_EARN is greater than BAL_ORG2_BEFORE — the order placed while company 2 was active credited company 2 pool. This is AC1 at the seam: earning follows the ACTIVE company, not the one the session started in {SPEC}
[REL] BAL_ORG1_AFTER equals BAL_ORG1_BEFORE — company 1 pool did not move while company 2 was the active one. This single equality is what separates a correct re-mint from a stale organization_id claim, and it is the assertion this case exists for {SPEC}
[STATE] ORDER_SPEND exists — the points-priced line was accepted and checked out against the pool of the company active at that moment, which is AC2 at the seam {SPEC}
[REL] ROWS_ORG2_AFTER is greater than ROWS_ORG2_BEFORE — the earn and the redemption are both recorded in the ledger the ACTIVE company members read {SPEC}
[STATE] ORG1_HAS_FOREIGN_ROWS is false — no transaction produced under company 2 is listed under company 1, so the two ledgers stayed separate in the read direction as well as the write direction {SPEC}
[REL] BAL_ORG2_AFTER_SPEND differs from BAL_ORG2_AFTER_EARN — the redemption moved the same pool the earn moved. Do NOT assert that it FELL: BL-LOY-010 forbids a points-only cart, so this order necessarily carries an earning cash line and the NET movement may be upward {SPEC}
